faceT -- THE PAIR FIXTURE. Minted 2026-09-01 (SEQ 113 item 3), Clay's charter.

WHAT IT IS FOR

Step 3 measured that the definition sweep at ruleActions.rtn:459 makes a COPY OF
A FIELD -- adopted=0 on 317 of 317 firings -- and that the copy SHARES the
original's GroupBody. Tony's definition, same day: a copy of a field is a field
with isCopy true whose body is shared with the original. A copy knows it is a
copy; the original does not know it has any.

So a field can have TWO FACES over ONE BODY, and the useful question is no longer
"is it a copy" but WHICH COLUMN a given piece of state lives in -- because the
body is shared and the field is not. This file asks that column by column.

⚠⚠ F1 IS THE ROW EVERYTHING ELSE RESTS ON, AND WITHOUT IT THE FILE ASSERTS
NOTHING. Every row below reads through two names. If those two names were the
same field, every "it round-tripped" would be trivially true and the fixture
would be a very confident tautology. F1 uses `addrOf` to show they are not:

    ADDROF faSrc field=#1 body=#2
    ADDROF faSrc field=#3 body=#2      DIFFERENT FIELD, SAME BODY

⚠ THE NUMBERS ARE THE POINT AND THE RAW ADDRESSES ARE NOT. `addrOf` prints a
per-run sequence number -- first distinct pointer is #1, and a pointer already
seen reads back its own number -- precisely so a fixture can pin identity. Raw
`%p` moves every run (measured: 0x104de3280 one run, 0x1035f1f00 the next) and
rule H3 forbids pinning a value that moves for correctness-unrelated reasons.
The numbers are stable across runs; the addresses are not.

WHAT THE COLUMNS DID

    F2  flags, FORWARD   write the original, read the face      1     SHARED
    F3  flags, REVERSE   write the face, read the original      1     SHARED
    F0  flags, clean     never written                     noPrinT    control
    F4  parent                                              unreadable

⚠ noPrinT LIVES IN GroupBody's `flags`, WHICH IS THE SHARED COLUMN. Asked and
answered for Clay: `GroupItem`'s `options` struct holds only `affiliation:2` and
`isCopy:1` -- noPrint is not in it. So the conditional in the charter resolves
the OTHER way: incant/roundTripT's ARM B2 was never this fixture's field-half
arm, it was already a BODY-half arm, and it reads 1 for the same reason F3 does.
What roundTripT lacks and this file adds is the identity proof, the FORWARD
direction, and the columns below.

⚠ BOTH DIRECTIONS ARE HERE ON PURPOSE. roundTripT B2 writes the twin and reads
the original. F2 does the opposite. A body that is genuinely shared must carry
the write either way, and a mechanism that happened to work in one direction only
would pass B2 and fail F2 -- which is a thing worth being able to find out.

⚠⚠ F4 IS RECORDED AS UNREADABLE, NOT AS A VERDICT, AND THE DISTINCTION IS THE
WHOLE OF THIS PROJECT'S DOCTRINE ABOUT VOID CONTROLS. `parent` is per-FIELD, so
the charter predicted it would not round-trip and this row was meant to show it.
It cannot: `faP1 = faSrc.parenT;` yields a field with no data, and a field with
no data returns its OWN TAG (bear-trap #26), so the row prints `faP1` -- the name
of the local it was captured into. That is not a reading of the parent column. It
is the absence of one wearing the shape of a reading.

    A PREDICTION THAT CANNOT BE MEASURED IS NOT CONFIRMED BY FAILING TO MEASURE
    IT. F4 is pinned at the tag echo so the row is honest and stays green; if a
    readable spelling for the parent column ever lands, F4 moves and someone
    reads this note.

⚠ THE OPTIONS AND rStuff COLUMNS ARE NOT HERE, FOR THE SAME REASON, and their
absence is deliberate rather than forgotten. `isCopy` is visible only through
`addrOf`, and `addrOf` reports the CARRIER -- every subject passed to an action
reads isCopy=1 including originals -- so it cannot report the subject's own bit.
An rStuff counter needs a live rule and a readable counter, and neither exists in
a form incant can ask today. Writing rows that echo their own tags would have
produced two more green rows asserting nothing.

⚠ BEAR-TRAP #35 GOVERNS EVERY READ IN THIS FILE. The first draft read
`faBag["faSrc"].noPrinT` and got `ORIG noPrinT` -- the chained subscript is
unreliable in print position. Every row reads through a BARE LOCAL captured with
`<-`, and that is not style: it is the difference between a measurement and a
plausible string.

⚠ F0 IS THE ANTI-VACUITY CONTROL AND IS RE-READ ON EVERY RUN. It reads the tag
`noPrinT` rather than 0, which is bear-trap #26 again and is the correct honest
answer for an unset flag. What matters is that it is NOT 1 -- if it ever were,
F2 and F3 would be asserting nothing at all.
